          "name": "Do architectural fins actually reduce cooling costs?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, when properly designed against a building's specific solar exposure, fins meaningfully reduce the direct solar heat gain reaching glazing and walls, lowering the overall cooling load compared to an unshaded elevation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I choose vertical or horizontal fins for my building?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on your building's orientation. Vertical fins work best against low-angle sun on east and west facing walls, while horizontal fins are more effective against high-angle sun on south facing elevations."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What material is best for architectural fins?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Aluminium is the most common choice due to its light weight and corrosion resistance, important since fins project outward and add structural load at their mounting point. Steel offers more strength but requires protective coating."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How is fin spacing determined for a specific building?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fin depth and spacing are calculated against the building's specific solar exposure and orientation, balancing shading performance against daylight access, rather than applied as a fixed generic pattern regardless of the building."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do fins need special structural engineering compared to flat panels?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, since fins are cantilevered and experience considerably more wind force than a flush panel of similar size, their mounting brackets and fixing details need specific structural calculation for the building's height, location, and fatigue resistance over time."
          }
